ITEMS = ((('what should i do for severe bleeding', 'how do i stop heavy bleeding', 'what is the first aid for bleeding', 'how to control bleeding from a wound', 'what to do when someone is bleeding badly', 'how do you handle serious bleeding', 'what helps stop bleeding quickly', 'how can i control bleeding safely', 'what is the best response to heavy bleeding', 'how should i treat a bleeding injury'), 'For severe bleeding, apply firm direct pressure with a clean cloth or dressing and keep it there. If blood soaks through, add more material on top and call emergency services. Stay with the person and keep them still and warm until help arrives.'), (('should i apply direct pressure to a bleeding wound', 'how long to hold pressure on bleeding', 'how firm should pressure be for bleeding', 'what if direct pressure does not stop bleeding', 'should i keep checking a bleeding wound', 'why is pressure used to control bleeding', 'can i use my hand to stop bleeding', 'how to press on a bleeding injury', 'what to do while waiting with bleeding', 'should i remove a blood soaked cloth while bleeding'), 'Direct pressure is the main way to control bleeding, using a clean pad and steady pressure. Do not keep lifting it to check, as that disturbs clots. Call emergency services if bleeding is severe or does not slow, and add more cloth rather than removing soaked material.'), (('what if blood soaks through the dressing during bleeding', 'should i add another dressing for bleeding', 'how to deal with soaked bandages from bleeding', 'what to do when bleeding continues through clothing', 'should i remove a soaked dressing after bleeding', 'how many layers can i add to a bleeding wound', 'what if the bleeding pad is full', 'how to keep pressure on a bleeding site', 'what to use for bleeding when i have no dressing', 'when to get help for soaked dressings from bleeding'), 'If blood soaks through a dressing, leave it in place and add another pad or cloth over the top, keeping pressure steady. Improvise with a clean piece of clothing if nothing else is available. Seek emergency help promptly if bleeding continues despite pressure.'), (('should i elevate a bleeding limb', 'how does raising a limb help bleeding', 'what if bleeding is from the leg', 'should i move someone with bleeding', 'how to position a person who is bleeding', 'is it good to lift an arm with bleeding', 'what if raising a bleeding limb causes pain', 'how to keep a bleeding person calm', 'should a bleeding person lie down', 'when not to move a bleeding person'), 'Keeping the person still and calm helps slow bleeding, and raising an injured limb may help if it does not cause pain. Avoid moving them if you suspect a head, neck or spinal injury. Call emergency services and keep the bleeding area under pressure.'), (('what are pressure points for bleeding', 'should i press on a artery to stop bleeding', 'how to use pressure points to control bleeding', 'where to press for severe bleeding', 'does pressing the arm stop bleeding', 'can pressure points control a bleed', 'what is the brachial pressure point for bleeding', 'should i try pressure points instead of direct pressure for bleeding', 'how long to hold a pressure point for bleeding', 'when to learn pressure point techniques for bleeding'), 'Pressure points are not a substitute for direct pressure on the wound, and you should not squeeze a limb tightly or cut off blood flow. Keep firm pressure on the bleeding site and call emergency services. Learn hands-on techniques through a recognised first-aid course.'), (('should i use a tourniquet for bleeding', 'when is a tourniquet needed for bleeding', 'how to apply a tourniquet safely for severe bleeding', 'can a belt be used as a tourniquet for bleeding', 'what are the risks of a tourniquet in bleeding', 'who should apply a tourniquet for severe bleeding', 'how long can a tourniquet stay on during bleeding', 'what if a limb is bleeding uncontrollably', 'should i loosen a tourniquet while bleeding', 'when to call for help with a tourniquet and bleeding'), 'A tourniquet is only for life-threatening bleeding that cannot be controlled by direct pressure. If you have been trained, use a purpose-made device and tell emergency services it is in place. Do not loosen it once applied, and never use improvised narrow straps.'), (('how do i tell if bleeding is arterial', 'what does spurting blood mean for bleeding', 'is bright red blood from a wound serious bleeding', 'what if blood is pumping from a wound with bleeding', 'how to respond to spurting bleeding', 'what is the difference between arterial bleeding', 'should i worry about the colour of blood during bleeding', 'what if bleeding pulses with the heartbeat', 'how urgent is spurting blood from a bleeding wound', 'when to call emergency services for spurting blood and bleeding'), 'Bright red blood that spurts or pulses suggests bleeding from an artery and is a medical emergency. Apply firm direct pressure at once and call emergency services. Keep the person still and warm, and be ready to give information about the injury.'), (('what to do for bleeding from the scalp', 'how to control bleeding from a cut on the head', 'should i press on a bleeding forehead', 'what if a head wound bleeds heavily', 'how to stop bleeding from a facial cut', 'can i dress a scalp wound that is bleeding', 'what if blood runs into the eyes while bleeding', 'how to protect the airway with head bleeding', 'should i tilt the head back for bleeding', 'when to seek help for head wound bleeding'), 'Apply gentle direct pressure to a bleeding scalp or facial wound with a clean cloth and call for help if it is heavy. Do not tilt the head back, as blood can run into the throat. Seek urgent assessment if the person is drowsy, confused or the wound is deep.'), (('what if there is something stuck in a bleeding wound', 'should i remove an object from a bleeding wound', 'how to stop bleeding around an embedded object', 'can i dress around a protruding object with bleeding', 'what if a knife is still in a bleeding wound', 'how to immobilise an object in a bleeding wound', 'should i push an object back in while bleeding', 'how to apply pressure around a foreign body that is bleeding', 'what to do until help arrives with an embedded object and bleeding', 'when to call emergency services for an impaled object with bleeding'), 'Do not remove an object embedded in a wound, as it may be holding back bleeding. Press gently around it and pad either side to keep it steady. Call emergency services and keep the person still until qualified help takes over.'), (('when should i call emergency services for bleeding', 'what are the signs of shock from severe bleeding', 'should i call an ambulance for heavy bleeding', 'how to know if bleeding is life threatening', 'what if a bleeding person turns pale', 'when to seek urgent help for a bleeding wound', 'what symptoms go with serious bleeding', 'how long can i wait before calling for help with bleeding', 'what if bleeding starts again later', 'should i stay with someone who is bleeding'), 'Call emergency services for bleeding that is severe, spurting, or will not slow with pressure. Pale skin, weakness, confusion, thirst or rapid breathing suggest shock. Stay with the person, keep pressure on the wound and do not give them food or drink.'))
